# @PROJECT@

Embedded project for **@BOARD_NAME@** (@MCU@) running **FreeRTOS V11.1.0**.
Peripheral access: pure-register style (no HAL).

## Layout

```
@PROJECT@/
├── src/                        application code (main.c creates tasks)
├── inc/                        headers (stm32l476_regs.h)
├── config/                     FreeRTOSConfig.h
├── startup/                    startup.c — vector table
├── linker/                     linker.ld
├── middleware/
│   └── FreeRTOS-Kernel/        the kernel (cloned at project creation)
├── build/                      .o / .d files (git-ignored)
├── bin/                        .elf / .bin / .hex / .map (git-ignored)
├── .embed/                     workflow metadata
└── Makefile
```

## The two tasks it starts

`src/main.c` creates:

- **`blink`** (prio 1, stack 128 words) — toggles P@USER_LED_PORT@@USER_LED_PIN@ every 500 ms
- **`hello`** (prio 1, stack 256 words) — stub task every 1 s (add UART later)

Plus hooks for stack overflow and malloc failure — both hit `bkpt #0` so your
debugger drops in immediately if either fires.

## Commands

Same as bare-metal: `make`, `make flash`, `make openocd`, `make debug`,
`make monitor`, `make clean`, `make ccdb`.

If the kernel dir is missing (e.g., fresh clone without submodules):

```sh
git clone --depth 1 --branch V11.1.0 \
    https://github.com/FreeRTOS/FreeRTOS-Kernel.git \
    middleware/FreeRTOS-Kernel
```

## Debugging FreeRTOS in GDB

Useful commands once you're in GDB (after `make debug`):

```
(gdb) info threads         # all tasks (with FreeRTOS GDB helper script)
(gdb) thread N             # switch to task N's context
(gdb) bt                   # backtrace for the task
(gdb) print pxCurrentTCB   # currently running task
(gdb) print uxSchedulerSuspended
```

For the full task list view, load `middleware/FreeRTOS-Kernel/tools/gdb-helper.py`
(not included here — grab it from the FreeRTOS-Kernel repo if you want it).

## Tuning

- **Heap size**: `configTOTAL_HEAP_SIZE` in `config/FreeRTOSConfig.h` (default 16 KB)
- **CPU clock**: `configCPU_CLOCK_HZ` — set to your actual clock after PLL setup
- **Tick rate**: `configTICK_RATE_HZ` (default 1 kHz)
- **Stack check**: `configCHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW = 2` (full pattern check) —
  turn to 1 or 0 if you need the cycles back

## Clock setup (important)

Right now `configCPU_CLOCK_HZ = 4000000` because the MSI runs at 4 MHz on reset.
When you write clock setup code (PLL → 80 MHz typical for L476), update this
value or your tick rate will be wrong.
